    "evidence_text": "Functional elements within the dynein microtubule-binding domain\n\nVirtually nothing is known regarding how binding affinity is achieved and modulated during ATP hydrolysis.\n\nThese suggest that nucleotide-sensitive affinity may be locally controlled at the site of contact.\n\nCharacterization of two classes of small molecule inhibitors of Arp2/3 complex\n\nCK-0993548 inserts into the hydrophobic core of Arp3 and alters its conformation.\n\nIdentification of Epstein-Barr virus nuclear antigen 1 protein domains that direct interactions at a distance between DNA-bound proteins.\n\nMicrotubule nucleation is an essential step in the formation of the microtubule cytoskeleton.\n\nHere, we explore the mechanisms by which androgen and Src regulate these processes and ask whether integrins play a role.\n\nStructural basis for the activation of microtubule assembly by the EB1 and p150Glued complex.\n\np53 binding to nucleosomal DNA depends on the rotational positioning of DNA response element.\n\nThe sequence-specific binding to DNA is crucial for the p53 tumor suppressor function.\n\nRibosomal protein S9 is a novel B23/NPM-binding protein required for normal cell proliferation.\n\nAlthough described as multifunctional, a genuine molecular function of B23 is not fully understood.\n\nWe found that S9 binding requires an intact B23 oligomerization domain.\n\nCdk5 regulates a variety of neuronal activities including migration, synaptic activity and neuronal death.\n\nNevertheless, small but distinct amounts of p35 and p39 were detected in the nucleus.\n\nIn particular, nuclear p35 and p39 were increased when the Cdk5 activity was inhibited.\n\nP446L-importin-beta inhibits nuclear envelope assembly by sequestering nuclear envelope assembly factors to the microtubules.\n\nA NE also forms over importin-beta-coated beads.\n\nCLIP-170 interacts with dynactin complex and the APC-binding protein EB1 by different mechanisms.\n\nIn contrast, the CLIP-170/EB1 interaction requires neither metal binding motif.\n\nThese results have implications for the targeting of both dynactin and EB1 to microtubule plus ends.",
